Infelation in Europe has slipped on food
Accelerated inflation, as measured in the euro area, is mainly due to the rapid increase in food prices.
In November, the increase in prices in the 13 euro area countries was higher than expected, after an inflation rate of 2.6 per cent in October, annual inflation of 3.1 per cent was recorded, according to the European Statistical Office located in Luxembourg.
The official forecasts indicated only 3 per cent, Bloomberg reported.
Contrary to the central banks of the United Republic of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland, the European Central Bank (ECB) has not lowered the policy interest by saying that lower interest, with commodity price growth and falling unemployment, would lead to generating an inflationary spiral.
The ECB would like to keep the rate of inflation below but close to 2 per cent.
According to one an analyst, this is Catch 22 for the ECB: 'to dexter' inflation must be deterred in order to no longer have to intervene on this subject at a later stage.
The inflation rate of 3.3 per cent in Germany is a record high over the last 12 years and in Spain has risen from 3,6 to 4.1.
Inflivity in the euro area took off due to the rapid increase in food and energy prices.
Cereal futures market ratings increased by 88 per cent, and soybean prices also reached its highest value since 1973.
The price of edible oil and dairy products also increased considerably in 2007.
This year, the global price of oil rose by 52 per cent, and last month a barrel of black gold cost almost .
According to the ECB in 2008, the rate of inflation will rise from 2.1 this year to 2.5 per cent this year, but in 2009 it will fall to EUR 1.9.
Analyst accounts for the 12-month inftration remain at around 3 per cent, however, for the next 3 to 4 months, the average annual rate will be 2.1 per cent.
At the same time, they expect the ECB to lower the policy rate twice in 2008.
Gallup indicates government crisis
After stagnating a long stagnator, the number of supporters of Fidesz (Young-Democracy Alliance) increased significantly in the early beginning of December, and since 2002 it currently holds the largest number of supporters, while Gallup (Pollifting Institute) has never seen such a low socialist support before, 13 per cent.
For loyal voters, due to the greater resolve of opposition voters would represent support of more than two-thousand (71 per cent) against the support of one-fifth (20 per cent) for MS-P (Hungarian Socialist Party).
According to the poll, MDF (Hungarian Democratic Forum) and the Alliance of Free Democrats are below parliamentarians: their support is two per cent across the population.
Out of loyal voters certain to vote, the number of supporters of both parties increased by 1 per cent. It has increased by 1 per cent.
The economic climate continued to deteriorate compared to the previous lowest point, the proportion of those who felt that the economic situation in the country was particularly serious had never been so high, 41 per cent, and the situation was quite serious, according to 46 per cent complementary.
After the more optimistic prospects of spring and last summer, the families are again more pessimistic about their financial situation.
For the next year, the majority of the population is expected to worsen and only one-by-tenth predicts an improvement in the situation.
Last December, 71 per cent of those surveyed, an unprecedented majority in previous Gallup polls, judged the Prime Minister to do his job rather poorly.
13 per per cent of respondents have confidence in Ferenc Gyurcs-ny, while 38 per cent support Viktor Orban.
54 per cent do not trust the Prime Minister at all and 27 per cent of the President of Fidesz.
An indicator of the change in the political situation is the proportion of society in the belief that discontagilization vis-à-vis the Prime Minister can be dealt with without a change of government.
At a time when the riots last October, 35 per cent of the respondents wanted the government and the Prime Minister to remain in office at the present time, the relative majority (33 per cent) believe that the holding of new elections would be the solution to this situation.
To the question concerning the intention of voting, 60 per cent of the respondents replied that they would certainly go to the vote and the additional 20 per cent said that they would probably go to vote.
To make the comparison, for an 'organic election next Sunday', only 55 per cent of the respondents would certainly participate and 15 per cent would probably take part; according to the current intentions, the result of the referendum there would be no doubt.
74 per per cent of people would say no to tuition fees in higher education, 79 per cent would say no at consultation costs and 84 per cent would say no to hospital fees.
When we look only at the same proportions among those who have expressed an intention to vote in the referendum, respectively 81, 84 and 89 per cent of the voters would reject the introduction of tuition fees, consultation fees and hospital subsistence.
Auchan is the dearest, Tesco increased its prices by nearly twenty per cent
Hyper-Marigries in the commercial triangle of Budaers maintained the average price increase of 16 per cent last year, with the difference that this year the VAT rate did not go up.
Article by Napi Gazdasog (Daily economy)
This year, in the commercial triangle of Budaers, the usual daily Christmas basket also increased by an average of 16 per cent, with an increase in value well above a thousand forints displayed in all hypermarkets.
'Extensive prices' in department food stores last December did not rise abruptly compared to the previous high base.
Nevertheless, consumers are, of course, shocked to see the fact that the items bought clearly below nine thousand forints last year now cost more than ten thousand forints.
The Daily Basket in the hypermarkets surveyed surpassed by at a minimum of 91 forint the psychological threshold of ten thousand forint.
And the highest value in the basket was 10,573 forints.
(In 2003, this value was 6,800 forint.)
Unicum and live fish prices remained almost at last year's level, but turkey, walnut and popp poppy prices rose sharply.
In shops, the price of flour and lounge followed data from the KSH (Central Institute of Satisfactory Statistics), but at Auchan only bread was found from 1 to 139 forint.
Competition did not lower the price of the product below 200 forints.
Astonishing to see, also in this hyper-Market, the price of the two-litre Coca-Cola bundled bottle was below 200 fornuzzles, so it cost only 257 forints, including the recorded bottle.
Nevertheless, this year, in terms of Daily Basket, Auchan was the most dearest and Cora the cheapest.
According to our calculations, Cora increased the price of its Daily Basket by 15 per cent, while Tesco and Auchan rose by about 18 per cent and 18 per cent respectively.
Comprising last year’s most cherished shopping cart and the cheapest basket of this year, we see a gap of 12 per cent, while the difference between last year’s cheapest and most cherished year is 21 per cent.
Prics increased on an average of 1,480 forints.
Over the past year, prices rose by an average of 1,206 forints, while knowing that the impact of the increase in the rate of VAT was already noticeable in last year's Christmas prices.
This process perpetuated trade, hypermarkets could not visibly determine which margin applied to products because they were unable to predict the impact of price change on consumption.
(If a trader miscalculates the turnover of a product, he risks losing a big profit on the margin adapted to trade intensity.)
As a result of the change in the rate of VAT, the increase in the prices of the Daily Basket has changed so excessively that the most expensive shopping basket was 10,720 fortints higher than the value in 2006, whereas the difference between the cheapest shopping baskets was only 844 forint.
This year, the difference was not so excessive since the value of the most cherished shopping basket was 1,630 Forints higher than last year, and the cheapest prices increased by 1,284 forts as well.
Bringing together the two seasons of Christmas, we saw a catastrophic increase in prices, in less than 20 months the prices of everyday consumer goods rose by more than 30 per cent in hypermarkets.
(This does not necessarily confirm KSH data: for some essential foods, the Institute of proficiency has noted an increase in prices by more than 30 per cent in 12 months.
This applies, e.g. flour and egg.
Depending on the Daily Basket, this sounds almost non-obsecutive since the price level of this product, already very expensive last year, rose by just more than 10 per cent compared to the base.
According to the clues, flour Suppliers occupy a stronger position than egg suppliers because they have succeeded in gaining public acceptance of the sharp increase in prices also by multinationals.
In the commercial Golden Triangle, hyper-Markets don't complain, they constantly record good years.
According to our experience, the majority of customers no longer look at prices in the three hypermarkets visited.
They buy everything in large quantities, they take long-preserving food in pallets.
On Wednesday morning, large tails were trained at the hustles of the three hypermarkets because the number of customers increased abruptly.
According to our information, there is less turnover in the provinces, many multinationals complain among polling companies of not even reaching the expected turnover, not only for food, but also for sustainable consumer goods.
Many hospitals save on disinfectants
This year, more than a thousand infections, caused mainly by the incorrect use of antibiotics, were recorded in hospitals.
Hygienic conditions of hospitals, insufficient beds and overburden of medical staff do not help to block infections, says the Chief Medical Officer of the National Epidemiology Centre.
Many hospitals are also sprinkling on disinfectants
Not long ago an MRSA infection was diagnosed at the Tragtology Department of Jonos.
Antivirative-resisting bacterium has been detected in a man hospitalised as a result of a serious accident.
The patient was lounged.
At the beginning of the year a similar infection occurred at the same location.
The injured person from a serious car accident came out of the hospital, but shortly afterwards had to return due to an MRSA infection and, since then, the doctors have not been able to do a major operation because of the bacterium that is found in his body.
As of December 10 this year, 1,094 hospital infections generated by a multi-drug-resistant pathogen were reported to the National System of Nosocomial Surveillance, said Karolina Borcz, Chief Medical Officer of the National Epidemiological Centre.
Knowing that this is the number of infections caused by a multi-drug-resistant pathogen that has actually been recorded in hospitals and for the rest we don't have any information.
Approximately two-thirds of infections (683 infections) were caused by MRSA, i.e. staphylococcus aureus resistant to methicillin-oxacillin.
One-third of people carry Staphylococcus aureus bacterium (SA) in their noses without developing a disease.
Infections caused by NAIs (such as angina, tonsillitis, cutaneous infections, traumatic infections) respond well to antibiotics and are easily curable.
However, if antibiotics are used excessively or inappropriately, the bacterium, which reacts well to antibiotics, will develop a bacterium well-resisting antibiotics that will proliferate rapidly in poor hospital conditions.
In the event of temporary or prolonged dysfunction of the immune system, this bacterium can cause serious hospitable infections, such as traumatic or spsepaemia, against elderly patients, small weights or a weakened organism.
According to the doctor-in-chief of the hospital, there is nothing special about MRSA infections, these infections are part of the day-to-day history of hospitals.
Above all, the infection must not be confused with the epidemic, you can't only find MRSA where you don't, said Tibor Nyulasi.' said Tibor Nyulasi.
SAIs are resistant to antibiotics.
'These patients are insulated, caregivers put rubber gloves and protective masks, and we have suitable antibiotics to treat them,' said Tibor Nyulasi.
Apparently, in-patients are examined if they carry MRSA, but infection cannot always be avoided.
Sample sampling is not fashionable
The vast majority of infections caused by MRSA could be prevented but the current conditions and circumstances are not favourable in Hungarian hospitals to prevent the development and proliferation of multi-drug-resistant pathogens.
Since this infection manifests itself mainly in hospitals and residences for the elderly, hospitals should monitor in-patients and immediately isolating MRSA sufferers.
But microbiological sampling is not fashionable in Hungarian hospitals.
It should be noted that the National Social Security Fund does not finance these examinations.
Most cases can only emerge if MRSA infection is detectable on the patient, said the Chief Physician of the Epidemiological Center.
The European Union had strict recommendations on the proper use of antibiotics in human medicine and there were Hungarian guidelines for the cure of certain infections and syndromes.
Efforts must be made to ensure that antibiotic therapy was targeted, i.e. microbiological examinations should be carried out in order to prevent drug resistance, but hospitals did not always take it into account.
Microbi microbiological sampling is even more rare in ambulatory care and in basic health care, Karolina said.
Many hospitals save on disinfectants
Scholastic evidence shows that MRSAs prolivating from one patient to another most often through medical personnel.
It is therefore essential to wash one's hands, gloves gloves and protective masks, but significant shortcomings are also detected in hospitals.
MRSA prolivates by infection transmitted by droplet inhalation and contact; however, due to the conditions in Hungarian hospitals, the infected patient cannot always be insuduced.
In addition, the rooms are often full, the beds are often too close to each other, while in terms of the epidemic, the patients should be at least 1.5 meters away from each other.
Unfortunately, we can also see that hospitals in a difficult financial situation are making savings on disinfectants, they lack nurses, there are many patients, so the risk of contamination is even greater.
According to Karolina Borzcz, in hospitals or preventive measures are respected, the number of infections is demotivably lower or the proliferation of the athmogenic agent can be blocked.
Government project against MRSA
The treatment of MRSA infection represents an exorbitant expenditure on social security coffers, which is why this infection is being combated everywhere.
In England, e.g. the government has established a programme to prevent or reduce the incidence of MRSA.
According to published data, this programme is effective and MRSA infections show a decreasing trend in England.
The European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control (ECDC) identifies among its priorities the prevention of the incidence of multidrug-resistant pathogens (such as MRSA) and the prevention of hospitable infections caused by these pathogens.
The curator, the author of a fatal accident, was not drunk
The official statement relating to the fatal accident caused by the Commissioner of the 22nd arrondissement has been prepared.
It can be ruled out that the 22nd arrondissement, resigned from his post, who crushed a pedestrian by causing his death last Tuesday would have led into drunk or stunned, said Attila Morvai, the spokesman for the Public Prosecutor's Office of the capital after the relative finding was made.
The finding confirmed the first result of the investigation, which already found that the ex-commissioner did not consume alcohol before driving.
Last Tuesday, the former head of the 22nd arrondissement police station crushed a 55-year-old man who found himself in front of him when he, with his private car, in Kossuth Lajos Street of the 22nd arrondissement.
(Besides, the fact that it crossed the road outside the pedestal crossing is not an exaggerating circumstance, according to the High-Lifting-Peace Code, a car starting a bend must allow pedestrians to pass.)
Pedestrian died after being taken to the hospital.
As a result of this accident, the Commissioner resigned from his post and called for his retirement because, according to the police communication, this accident shocked him a great deal.
Investigating this case is being carried out by the Fsearch in Budapest.
Attila Morvai also mentioned that the ex-commissioner cooperated at the beginning with the magistrates conducting the investigation.
Additional EUR 400 million for infrastructure
Hungarian Minister of Finance Jonos Veres and Vice-President Marta Gajecka of the European Investment Bank (EIB) in Budapest signed on Friday in Budapest three agreements worth EUR 400 million to support education, transport and the environment infrastructure as well as energy.
In 2006, the European Investment Bank (EIB) adopted a EUR 300 million budget for Hungarian budgetary financing of transport and environmental projects to be carried out from the Cohesion Fund whose credit contract worth EUR 100 million has already been signed by the parties; this time the signed contract concerns the use of the remaining EUR 200 million.
The EUR 150 million credit agreement also covers the budgetary financing of education-related projects.
The EIB has approved a total amount of EUR 300 million for Hungary and the remaining EUR 150 million can be used at a later date following the extension of the credit agreement.
The parties have signed an EUR 50 million contract for innovation, the state budget will use this envelope primarily as part of the research and development project at the University of Debrecen.
During the negotiations, Metro Line 4 project was also discussed.
The parties agreed that they would redisease the initial contract when Hungary has finalised the details of metro financing with the EU.
This could happen in the first quarter of 2008.
Since 1990, a total of EUR 8.4 billion in the EIB has allocated EUR 8.4 billion for the financing of 113 projects in Hungary.
The EIB provides its loans at the EURIBOR related interest rate, with a grace period of 8 years and without commitment commission.
Bulgaria as an EU real-estavity market paradise
The development of the Bulgarian real-estimate real-estimate real-estimate real-fashioned Bulgarian real-estate real-estate market, the attention of the whole of Europe is oriented towards this country of the Balkans little mentioned in the past.
"Which" and who bought in this EU market which is always the cheapest but which will quickly become dear?
Analysis of the Bulgarian Re/Max Division.
At the present time, in real estate, Bulgaria is undeniably the most fashionable target in Eastern Europe with ski apartments and low-cost seaside houses.
In the mountains, the most popular regions are Bansko, Borovec, Pamprovo and Vitosa and, by the sea, Albéna, "Golden Sands", "Coast of the Sun", the former Sozopol and Nessebar, but there are more and more of them looking for a building in the Kavarna and Balcik region.
According to Analysts from Re/Max Bulgaria, sooner or Late, the central region of Veliko Tarnovo, Gabrovo, Elena, Triavna will also arouse the interest of investors and there is also an increasing number of investors buying in the inexpensive area of Ruse.
Sofia, the capital, also offers attractive opportunities, the areas of shops and desks promise the highest amortisation rate.
Bulgaria is on the best track to become one of the most attractive golf havens in Europe and thus to be a priority destination, after the summer season and the winter season, also in the other two seasons of the year.
More vigilant Investors are already buying in large numbers, e.g. around Bansko.
Analysts believe that people buying a building in Bulgaria at the same time seek to make a difference in their life, their way of life.
Most of the buyers are English, but others come from France, Belgium, from Spain and more and more from Romania.
Maltese and Cypriot Investigators are also present: on the Black Sea and in the most reemitted ski resorts we traditionally meet a large number of Russian buyers.
Foreign buyers are divided into three groups, says Georgij Jankov, Regional Director of Re/Max Bulgaria.
People who intend to settle buy renovated buildings, at a price varying between EUR 50 and EUR 100 000, or dilapidated Old houses, at a price ranging from EUR 5 to EUR 15, which are then renovated.
The criteria include easy access and communication opportunities, proximity to a major city, shops and, in general, the local doctor.
The second group includes buyers of second-graders (Since last December, Bulgaria is ranked among the top 10 vacation destinations in Germany).
The third group is made up of commercial investors who purchase in the hope of achieving capital gains in the medium or long term.
The price of buildings in the capital is constantly increasing as a result of increasing demand.
In the European Union, house prices are the lowest in Bulgaria; however, over the last 5 years they have risen by 21 per cent every year.
Raising real estate prices mainly exceeds the increase in wages.
Fodor and six per cent Hungarian
According to the Communication of the Minister for the Environment and the Water, the Minister for the Environment and the Ministry of the Environment and the Ministry of Waters is coming to an end on Tuesday, however, Wednesday, after the official opening, negotiations by ministers on the international climate regime after 2012 will continue.
Experts and politicians arriving from 192 countries around the world will discuss the Bali road map, which is intended to define the efforts to be made after the emission reduction period defined by the Kyoto Protocol up to 2012.
At the present time, there has been a great deal of discussion on this between developing and developed countries.
The United States also pointed out that, for the time being, it did not wish to discuss the exact figures, while it was prepared to sign an agreement on reducing greenhouse gas emissions from 25 to 40 per cent from the 1990 level until 2020.
Another important topic of the conference was whether developed countries were committed, excluding emission reductions, to providing financial and technological assistance to countries in need.
An agreement has also been signed on the aid to be supplied to developing countries.
The aid fund defined in the Kyoto Protocol will be managed by an Executive Board composed of 16 members probably under the United Nations Global Environment Fund (GEF), said Piac és Profit (Market and Profit).
Six per cent in Hungary
On behalf of Hungary, Minister for the Environment and the Ex-Lispr of the Environment and the Ministry of the Water, the Minister for the Environment and the Ministry of the Environment and the Ministry of the European Liberal and Rural Development, the European Commission, confirms the EU's ambition to retain its role as a flagship ship in the global efforts aimed at protecting the climate and encouraging the signing of the new international agreement before 2009.
The minister explained that the EU is committed to reducing its greenhouse gas emissions by 20 per cent by 20 per cent below the level reached in 1990, regardless of the successful signing of the 'Sex 2012 Agreement', and it was prepared to reduce them by up to 30 per cent if the other major regions of the world and/or the developing countries were also prepared to make commitments tailored to their responsibility and capabilities.
Fodor pointed out that in 1997 the countries signatories to the Kyoto Protocol targeted a total reduction of 5.2 per cent, and all of the 15 members of the EU at that time made a commitment out of 8 per cent fewer.
Hungary has made a commitment to reduce greenhouse-gas emissions by 6 per cent compared to the period between 1985 and 1987, our country will fulfil its commitment, recalls the press release of the Ministry of the Environment.
What is at stake is high in Bali because the long-term international agreement on climate policy must be put in place within two years, otherwise the entry into entry into force of the agreement would certainly be pushed back after 2012, in this case the reduction commitments below the values provided for in the Kyoto Protocol would not be immediately followed by a new international emission-reducing system whose need has been highlighted in the new report of the International Science, the International Science Council.
The emission reduction would be delayed, and an increase in the Earth's average temperature of more than 2 degrees celcius could no longer be avoided which, according to the scientists, had extremely serious and irrevocable consequences.
The British Army Recruits YouTube
The units of the British army are transmitting on the video-sharing site recordings made during its operations in Iraq and Afghanistan with the aim of making themselves popular.
Firstly, it is the British Air Force (RAF) who has been passionate about YouTube: the special units involved in Afghanistan are practicing video clamp on operations carried out against the Taliban since March 2006, the BBC announced.
These recordings, taken using helmet-mounted mini-channel cameras, are monitored at Air Force Operations Headquarters in Kandahar and are then relocated to Londrès where compatriots charge them on the Internet.
The political message is inescapable
Soldiers of the Air Force have already spent 139 footstretches on the net through this route and their "string" has been watched by more than 75,000 people.
The British Ministry of Defence listed as "defenceheadquarters" in the spring of 2007 and reports on operations in Iraq and in Afghanistan, while the Commonwealth Ministry (Commonwealth Office) and international peacekeeping forces in Iraq (MNFI) also have their own channels on YouTube.
Mission to Afghanistan
Depending on the original purpose of the Air Force, the videos were screened to recruit; according to the Ministry of Defence, it was also used to inform the general public about the real objectives of the army's presence abroad.
Propaganda published in this way costs virtually nothing compared to the usual recruitment advertisements placed on television, communicated to BBC Gordon Mackenzie, a defence consultant; on the other hand, it also inevitably bears a political message, since it justifies in a special form the validity of the military operations decided by the government.
The U.S. made a game
The army is annoyed because, apart from carefully composed and censored videos, amateur videos are placed on the sharing site which also present, for example, the death of soldiers: in “official” propaganda films a little hypocrites, officers do not avoid talking about losses, but they never present the bloody side of the fight.
By the way, a large number of videos had already been on the net showing the areas of operation in Iraq and Afghanistan that were being carried out on an actual mission, but the British army is only rushing for the loading of these videos for a short time.
The U.S. recruit a little bit differently by using America's Army shootout game that can be downloaded free of charge. In this game, the player can put themselves directly in the snap-square of soldiers as part of a more or less real-world simulation.
Novel raffle and doughnuts in the ministries
The Minister of Labour, M. Lamperth, does not seem to be sentimental: instead of celebrating Christmas, she will hold an end-of-year meeting with her collaborators who will be able to bid farewell to the year of 2007 by taking doughnuts and coffee.
Another ministry will organize a party with live music, yet another will delight civil servants with a raffle and offer a drink to create a Christmas ambience.
Celebration, Christmas ambience, concerts and buffet, thus entertains power.
The public authorities are also preparing for Christmas.
This year, the biggest Christmas party will be organized by the Ministry of Defence, but it is not the employees of the ministry who will be invited.
At Christmas, Orphelins, the children of fallen soldiers will receive gifts for missionary Christmas, the family members of the soldiers currently serving in the foreign missions are invited and a press reception will also take place.
The Ministry is providing a budget of 25.0 million HUF to create the end-of-year ambience.
In comparison with other government departments, the National Development Agency and the Ministry of Economics and Transport (GKM) will fallocate.
The GKM feat will take place in the House of the Future with a total budget of 4,320,000 forints and, out of the six hundred employees roughly, three hundred and fifty are awaited.
At this feat, the Christmas ambience will be required, there will be actors and also a raffle.
The Noel of NFz costed 4,700,000 HUF and was hosted on Wednesday in the National Council of Justice (ILO) building with concerts and a dinner.
Leasing of the hall has costs a million forints, the rest has been spent on more than four hundred guests.
Depending on our information, an NFC service is invited to another Christmas party which is organised with European money by the National Centre for Economic Development.
The Théâtre de la Gaîté building was rented for these purposes.
This information hasn't been corroborated by MAG as we have not been able to contact the manager.
Suit isn't mandatory
The United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees (ILO) did not only host the NF-YES, the Ministry of Justice and the General Assembly of the Federal Public Police, which, instead of a Christmas party, organized a ball on St. Luca on 7 December.
The United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights did not charge them for the use of its rooms.
This ball numbered five hundred people.
The feivity began with the minister's words of welcome and the dinner was followed by a da dancing evening on live music.
Answer to [origo's question: evening dress was not at all mandatory at this ball.
The Ministries of Health, Labour and Local Self-Conferentials are preparing for a sober party.
The Ministry of Health will organize a show and a party to its employees, within its walls, for 400,000 Hint.
On the other hand, the Minister of Labour, M. Lamperth, did not officially invite his collaborators to a Christmas party but at a year-end working meeting.
One hundred and eighty people are expected who will be invited to have a coffee and doughnut, the press service said, the party will only cost 335 000 HUF.
Gordon Bajnai, Minister of Local Government, will simply invite his collaborators to have a drink from the Ministry of Local Government and Spatial Planning.
The Ministry of Finance (PM) will organize a Christmas party in its large room at which 436 employees are awaited.
It intends to spend 2,500 forints per guest with a buffet and a glass.
Since the arrival of Jonos Veres, PM employees celebrate Christmas every year in the same way.
No fencing atmosphere, the official character is reinforced not only by the Minister's briefing but also by the festivion time taking place in the afternoon.
The Ministry of Agriculture will organise a reception on 20 January to celebrate the New Year, the Christmas party will not take place.
Reception will be held at Vajdahunyad Castle, located in Bois de Cottage, and according to the communication of the Ministry, 1000 Hints per person are calculated for the 100 guests.
Depending on the press service, this is going to be a very simple cocktail to which only foreign diplomats accredited in Hungary will be invited.
These cocktails, organized every each year, are opened with a few words of welcome from the minister followed by a modest meal.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs and the Ministry of Education and Culture did not celebrate at all for Noel, the respective press services communicated to the respective press services.
The Ministry of the Environment's press service did not know the official Christmas price.
However, more than four hundred people were invited to the party at the Museum of Decorative Art.
Mega-strike forces come together
The Unions of the League and the National Association of Workers' Councils will, on Friday, constitute their strike commission, which will be in charge of coordinating the unindeterminate strike on Monday.
On Saturday, a demonstration general assembly will be held, and on Monday, the day of the final vote on the Health Count Act, the strike will begin.
This action will no doubt cause major traffic junctions because rail traffic will be stopped almost entirely and the employees of BKV (public transport company) will also go on strike until 1 p.m.
The league trade unions will announce Friday afternoon the details of their Monday strike, organized with the National Association of Workers' Councils on Friday afternoon.
LIGA - MOSz's Strike Commission will also be set up, it will serve as the coordinator of Monday's actions.
On Saturday, a General Assembly of Protests will be held, starting on Monday, organisations joining the strike will stop the work for an indeterminate period if they cannot reach an agreement with the government.
This means that the date of the end of the strike, starting from the day of voting in parliament on the Health Counts Act, is not defined.
If the National Assemblie does not enact the Health Counts Act, trade unions will immediately withdraw the call for a strike.
On the other hand, if the law is passed, the strike committees will meet and decide on the action.
According to Monday's agenda of the National Assemblie, it is likely that voting on the Health Coffee Act takes place only later in the evening or even at night.
As a result of the strike scheduled for Monday, major traffic jams are planned: the railways will stop working for an indeterminate period of time and during the morning part of the public transport in Budapest and more than half of the Volin companies are in danger of being shut down.
At the same time, at different points in the country, half of the lane will also be stalled.
Many trade unions and civil organizations have joined the call for a strike, a list of which is continuously updated on the league's website.
Some organizations are reporting an on-going strike for an indeterminutilized basis, while others have declared a temporary stoppage of work.
Until Thursday afternoon, the Hungarian Iron Eminister (Hungarian Railways) and the Free Shikers' Free Trade Union (VDS-S) did not succeed in reaching an agreement on the minimum services, but, as the Christmas holidays approached, more and more people took the train.
According to the current communications, public transport in Budapest will go on from the beginning of service until 1 p.m., Budapest airport between 7 a.m. and 9 a.m., the League’s Electricity and Thermal Energy Industrial Trade Union will go on a four-hour strike, the Teachers’ Union and the Teachers’ Democratic Syndicate called for a strike for 2 hours and from 2 a.m. to 8 a.m.
The National Physician's Medical Association will go on an alternating basis throughout the country, the Union of Health and Social Sector Employees (EDDS) attached to the S-E-EF will carry out a two-hour warning strike.
The most important of the four demands, formulated by the league, demands that the government withdraw its proposal for health-reform and submit a new approach to the social debate.
The second claim requires that the new method of calculating the pension to be put in place as from next year be introduced in several stages.
The third demand calls for a pre-sentencing guarantee of retirement before the legal age, which would make it possible to pass into the system the functions that deserve it before 2010.
The fourth reason for the action is the rejection by the Free Mermaid of Railway Workers (VDS-S), attached to the League Syndicates, the proposal of the Ministry of the Economy and Transport concerning secondary railway lines and the waiting time for another proposal.
Political Capital: The Hungarian Guard Raised Country Risk
According to the Risk Analysing Division of Political Capital (Risk Analysing Division), the activity of the Hungarian Guard formed last August with the support of Jobbik Magyarorszanger Mozgalom (Mount for Best Hungary) increases the political risk of Hungary and the region in several respects.
In its “risk warning”, the company points out that, in connection with the activity of the Hungarian Guard, the greatest political risk already is that “in the short term aggressive ethnic clashes may occur as a result of the ever-increasing ethnic tense between the Gypsy and non-Gy Gypsy population in Hungary”.
The research institute added that the political strategy of the Hungarian Guard, which is probably aimed at pronouncing this situation is the main reason.
As they say, this is explained by one of the main facsiwers of Hungarian political actors: “public actors have gone through the public debate on the Roma case to the representatives of the Radical and Extreme political groupings who have based a political strategy on the dispute between the Gypsies and the Hungarians”.
As a result, there is an increase in the problem, normal dialogue becomes impossible, extremists are being stepped up and the arrival of aggressive conflicts is more likely, they say.
However, according to the research institute, due to the increased attention of the Hungarian and international media the activity of the Hungarian Guard “significantly damaged the image of Hungary abroad, in particular with regard to confidence in the stability and democratic ripeness of the country”.
They point out that, as a result of greater publicity given to the reorganisation of radical and extreme Hungarian right-wing groups since the autumn of 2006, many associations have been formed in Hungary since in the summer of 2007 being known as “National Self-Defence Association”.
“The common trait of these rallies is that they define themselves as a civil organization when they publicly declare the preparation of their members for defence missions (discreastrophes, national defence, defence of the order), armed if necessary, moreover, evoking the unsatisfiedness of the police, of the army, in other words of the state”, they added.
They pointed out that everything went according to the method which, on the basis of the resentment of political society and the parties, sought to disguise different political ambitions under a civil aspect.
The emphasis on civilian character and the status of an association therefore serves only only to avoid the legal pitfalls of registering these associations in the court, but they also constitute a political strategy.
The analysis points out that the activity of the Hungarian Guard is a factor that also increases the risk compared to neighbourhood relations.
“In recent months, many neighbouring countries cite the Hungarian Guard as a source of potential risk, and ultra-nationalist political actors in some neighbouring countries wish to justify their anti-Hungarian policy by referring to the constitution of the Hungarian Guard.
This has an impact on political stability in the region,” they added.
According to the research institute, the constitution of the Hungarian Guard is not a unique phenomenon in Eastern Central Europe; political risks have increased for the region as a whole.
As they say, the functioning of the Hungarian Guard resembles, for example, the activity of the Bulgarian National Guard whose constitution was declared by the Bulgarian National Union (BNU) in August 2007.
The large number of Roma disturbances that have erupted in an area of Sofia (Kraszna Poljana) are at the origin of the constitution of the Bulgarian Guard.
By taking advantage of these events, the United Nations Office on Drugs and Northern Ireland decided to establish a guard by stating that the objective of the organization is “national self-defence”.
In their view, over the past 17 years, gypsies have “terrified” Bulgaria, but the state has remained inactive and neutral, Political Capital recalls.
They added that the initiative gave rise to lively debates in Bulgarian public opinion.
As they say, the declaration of certain Bulgarian Roma organisations concerning the formation of their own guard as resistance indicates an increase in the risk of ethnic clashes.
The functioning of the Hungarian Guard showing similarities to that of the Bulgarian National Guard does not preclude Hungarian Roma from reacting the threat in the same way by organizing their own self-defense, according to the Health Capital risk warning.
At the telegraphic agency's request for information, Attila Juhasz, research director of Political Capital, said: risk analysis is one of the activities of the research institute.
As he said, their risk analysis is published every year, and if events occur that could have an impact on the change in the risk, they inform the public, as in the present case, by issuing a hazard warning.
The festivity isn't marked red everywhere
In Hungary, the law precisely defines festive days and, around the festivities, it has become common to reorganize working-passing working-days, the number of which will decrease next year.
Holidays are non-uniform in the EU and in the United States either.
The Hungarian legislation distinguishes between national and state holidays as well as rest days.
Ranking days are often related to religious holidays or important historical events.
In Hungary, we are celebrating the new year, the last day of the year that is coming to an end and the first day of the new year are also days of rest, especially so that they can rest quietly.
On the other hand, in one year's time, we have a total of eleven celebrations marked in red.
March 15 is a national holiday in Commemorating the Revolution of 1848, such as on 23 October which is the commemorative day of the 1956 revolution.
On 20 August, we are commendating our state, on 1 May is Labour Day and on 1 and 1 and 2 November, we will remember the members of our deceased family and friends.
In addition, we have two days at Easter, Pentecost and at Christmas.
On the days of our state, national or religious festivities rarely falling over a weekend, depending on the calendar, it has become common to reordinate the working days in cases where only one working day stays before or after the holiday marked in red.
It's true that we're paying for extended weekends by working or going to school on the Saturday preceding the extended weekend.
For many years, the Minister of Labour has been acting in a particular regulation on replacement days in the days before or after the festivities.
The law extends for all employers and their employed employees, on civil servants and on persons working in the public service.
It does not, however, apply to service companies, institutions rotating without interruption and in service, by virtue of their vocation, and also for rest days.
This year, working days will be changed only around the holidays at the end-of-year: as of 24 December, life stops almost throughout the country because, depending on the experience, the majority of people spend more overtime or take holidays that they have saved for these purposes so that they can spend the days between Christmas and New New Year's Day at home.
Next year a lot of festivities fall in the middle or at the end of the week, so there will be less changes in working days compared to this year.
The calendar will be reorganised for the first time around Labour Day to allow employees to stay at their home for four consecutive days.
Saturday, 25 April will be a working day for a rest day on Friday, 2 May.
Another change is planned for 23 October: Saturday 18 October will be a replacement working day on Friday.
On the other hand, next year, the end of the year will be celebrated because from 24 December, everyone will take a non-stop break even if New New Year's Day falls upon a Wednesday.
