Billion-yivated perfidious trader because of its inferiority complex
Concealant fraudster.
For example, its former employee is the French giant, Société générale, the owner of the Czech banked bank.
The bank lost almost EUR 5 billion because of it.
Rather, Kerviel seems to be just a great player who wanted to match his colleagues, and who, in addition, can in no way be considered incompetent.
Unlike all major bankers, he recognized the US Mortgage Market Crisis in a timely manner.
"It is fragile", his superiors cheered to report it to the police.
However, according to Kerviel's deposition, reported by the French media, including Le Monde, this man's transactions produced at the end of last last year a profit of EUR 1.6 billion.
The crisis goes beyond the United States
'Everything I wanted was to make money for my bank,' he said in the deposition.
Last summer, it managed to take advantage of the US crisis to earn half a billion euros.
In fact, he felt an opportunity from the very beginning of the year.
Information began to emerge in February, according to which so-called “sub-prime” mortgages granted to low-income clients or to those who had experienced repayment difficulties in the past would have problems.
But this crisis should not affect the economy as a whole.
Kerviel didn't believe in it and he bet well.
Thus, at the end of the year, its profits amounted to EUR 1.6 billion.
"As soon as I won my first half-bill, I didn't know how to announce it," Kerviel said and added that he didn't, of course, keep anything for himself."
Throughout the interrogation, he confesses to skewing the reports in order not to be detected.
He managed to deceive his superiors, although the Eurex Scholar had begun to take an interest in his transactions.
Who doesn't risk anything
Was he afraid of being discovered?
Pretty different.
"I felt that the simple fact of getting 1.6 billion for the bank would satisfy them."
Where does its desire to risk such a great risk come from?
He reached his position only gradually.
He started working at Société Générale in 2000 just after finishing graduating at the University of Lyon.
Its annual salary revolved around 51,000 dollars.
Two years Later, he was an assistant trader and, in 2005, he became an independent trader with an annual salary of 90,000 dollars.
Yet he knew that he would never earn as much as the others.
"From my first interview in 2005, I knew I was far less regarded than others.
In terms of my academic background and my professional and personal background," Kerviel told investigators.
However, shortly after taking office, it achieved its first great success.
Thanks to Allianz equities, it earned half a billion euros by betting on the fall of this title during the attacks in Londrès.
And the mechanism was unleashed -- he wanted more.
As long as you win, we'll leave you in peace
"There was like a snowball effect," he told the police.
But the superiors were aware of everything, or rather, they doubted it.
"Until I won, they were pretending to close my eyes."
However, it is then that at the beginning of the year comes.
It loses EUR 1 billion in one day.
In mid-January, after the weekend, he decided to hide the money.
On Monday, when he arrives at work, he is no longer employed by the bank.
A few days later, the bank announced that it had lost EUR 5 billion because of it.
And how did we discover it?
He became a suspect because of his vacation.
"Life last year, I took only four days' vacation.
A trader who doesn't take a vacation is a trader who doesn't want to leave his book to another", concludes Kerviel.
The Chew has an opportunity to obtain EUR 365 billion
Yesterday, the Ministers of the Czech Government signed a total of nine operational programmes with representatives of the European Commission, which could bring EUR 14 billion (approximately 365 billion kroon) to the Czech Republic in the coming years.
This is about half of the total sum that the Czech Republic can receive from Brussels in the years 2007 to 2013.
Nearly DKK 125 billion resulting from seven operational programmes will be allocated to the Czech regions.
Czech companies will be able to obtain around DKK 100 billion as part of the Enterprise and Innovation programme.
The transport sector will benefit most, up to EUR 150 billion.
The transport programme is the most important operational programme and accounts for up to 22 per cent of the financial resources that the Czech Republic can obtain from these projects.
This money will lead to an increase in the budget of the National Transportation Infrastructure Fund, which will manage NOK 90.8 billion next year.
The Enterprise and Innovation Programme for SMEs and Entrepreneurs must be used to obtain financial resources to encourage innovation within the company.
Firms should obtain financial resources for the introduction of new technologies, research in industry, but also for the introduction of new and more effective management methods.
Until now, the Czech Republic has been one of the newest beneficiaries of EU funds.
The General Court confirmed the sentence of 20 years'imprisonment for the former President of Nicaragua
The Nicaraguan Court of Appellate on Thursday upheld 20 years'imprisonment for the former country President Arnold Alemon, to which he was convicted in 2003 for a fraud of 100 million dollars (near NOK 1.8 billion).
Later he was discharged and lived under house arrest in his hacienda close to the Nicaraguan capital.
Whether or not the former head of this Central American state, in office between 1997 and 2002, will be incarcerated again is, according to the AP agency, not clear at the present time.
According to Court of Appeal President Ileana Pérez, everything is now going to depend on the prison officers, either they will let Aleman finish his sentence at his home, or go to prison again.
Four years ago, the Tribunal found that Alemun regularly shifted government money to foreign company accounts in Panama.
These were fictitious companies, and the accounts actually belonged to members of their families and closest supporters.
Some of this money went into the funds that Alemon had created to finance its election campaign in 2001.
The budget was adopted
That's how it is.
The State budget has been adopted.
How is this budget exceptional?
It is the first budget designed by this government and accepted by the governmental majority without support from the Opposition.
The one for the current year passed a year ago thanks to the supporter of the Czech Socialist Party - SSD, since it was Minister Sobotka who had prepared it and SSD couldn't afford its refusal.
Contrary to the turbulent debate preceding the vote on the declaration of defiance to the government, this time nothing drafficiated.
The Chamber of Deputies has without any problems adopted this law, which, although the most involuntarily, is usually neglected.
This is a kind of paradox.
Wrong whether we like it or not, each chapter and every article is the beginning and the end of any government.
The budget is a constituent part of the policy, although hidden behind the competition of power between government and opposition.
The latter is more attractive in the media, and that's why it's given more attention in the media.
Politician disputes are much more relevant to public opinion rather than abrupt figures.
The national budgets of past socialist governments suffered from three evils that the current budget is not capable of regulating at the same same time:
The first is the important part of the mandate fees imposed by the law, consisting not not only of pensions, but also of social security contributions, adopted last year by the quiequent "coalition" bringing together the SSD parties.
The second is an indefranchisement, as a consequence of the first evil.
The third obstacle, on the other hand, is the public finance deficit, i.e. the deficit of cities and municipalities.
However, it is not the deficit of the State budget that represents the greatest risk.
It is public and relatively under control.
The real ticking tumult is the deficit of cities and municipalities.
Many mayors often stubbornly issue bonds, embroidering disproportionate their municipalities in order to obtain new sources of new sources of investment, excluding money from taxes, tax fees and bank loans.
In the past few years, they managed to repay these debts through the mass sales of the shares of distribution systems, particularly those obtained from the State in connection with privatization.
Nowadays, they no longer have this source.
If one confines itself to official and ver Verifiable facts, it can be seen that:
Our economy for several years now, just like the rest of the world, a phase of growth and there is not much in need of research.
It was a unique opportunity for the successive governments of this party to regularize public finances.
Instead, however, they were indemnifying the State more and more.
Other countries, apart from a few exceptions such as Hungary, Albania or Egypt, have handled their affairs in a much more reasonable manner than our Republic.
It is a monstrous stitch that essentially falls on previous governments.
We are among the countries on a global scale that are least threatened by poverty.
We therefore do not need any massive social programmes, which in the last few years have unfortunately become unnecessarily swollen in recent years.
Although we have succeeded in suppressing an absurd populist expenditure, approved last year prior to the elections, if a lag in the retirement age gap, as well as a reform of the pension system will not take place in the near future, then any policy will cease to exist in that country and whoever is in power, it will only administer.
Twiwice exaggerating, it can be said that politicians in power will become bureaucrats mechanically distributing the majority of money between pensions and social benefits.
That is why the current budget cannot be revolutionary, but it can only suspend the mechanism whereby ordinary costs are repulsed by mandate fees.
The deficit of EUR 70 billion projected for 2008 would be absolutely useless if previous governments did not dissipate all the money they could.
If we really don't really want the policies in power to become mere  bureaucrackers, we have to be patient and give this fragile governmental coalition, aware that the state can only spend the equivalent of what it manages to levy in taxes and tax duties the quiet necessary for the preparation of new reform laws.
It is only the latter which will make it possible to gradually achieve a balanced State budget.
Jio Krej
Banks Want to Settlement the Crise
The US Federal Reservation Bank, the European Central Bank and the central banks of England, Switzerland and Canada have decided to jointly tackle the financial-market crisis.
More than USD 100 billion will thus be placed in money markets through auctions.
It will be a question for the banks to sell, through an embedding system, the interest rates at which they are willing to borrow money.
By way of example, the U.S. Fed will auction 40 billion dollars and, in addition, it will provide EUR 24 billion to the European and Swiss Central Banks.
This is the first time since the attacks of 11 September that the Central Banks of Individual States have decided to take concerted action aimed at preventing or even diminishing the effects of the financial crisis.
'This approach tends to solve the major problems of the economy, i.e. the growing lack of free capital that banks could lend,' writes in his report to clients Bernard Baumohl of the consulting company Economic Outlook.
'Serious action by the central banks has reduced the probability of a recession in the United States from 65% to about 50 per cent,' adds Baumohl in its report.
Banks have already lost USD 60 billion due to unreimbursed loans, especially in the United States.
Analysts are still waiting for further losses in billions.
'If the central banks have chosen this approach, it is because they have to really comprehend what still happens,' says ABN Chief Economist Amro Kieran Davies.
'It seems that the situation is really bad,' he adds.
The Central Banks are concerned that if the banks themselves have difficult access to the loans, they will lend more dearly to their customers.
This would reduce consumption and plunge the US economy into a recession that could influence economic growth around the world.
The Central Banks made this choice after it was not possible to reduce the inter-bank rate of Londrès, called LIBOR, used to borrow on the interbank market.
This meant that the banks were still afraid of mutual borrowing, despite the fact that the United States Central, Canadian and English Banks had repeatedly lowered their interest rates during the autumn.
'This will have the same effect as a further decline in interest rates,' Tempus Consulting told Reuters Greg Salvaggio.
Some analys analysts do not believe that central banks’ approach will help acquiesthetize the crisis, since it does not solve the main problem of the fall in the US housing market.
President-in-Chittleneck: Economists’ Debates Are Unbelievable
Global climate change is supposed to be detrimental mainly to developing countries.
President Vaclav Klaus has re-pronounced the problem of global global warming.
Criticism this time befilised at the United Nations International Conference on Climate Change culminating in Bali.
In Vienna, where he unveiled his book The Blue Planet, not a green one, he stated that the debates of ecologists were unbelievable.
According to Klaus, it is not only the structure of the conference, a curious mixture of representatives of governments and non-governmental organisations, which is unacceptable, but also the basic difference between the rhetoric that accompanies it and the reality.
'The debate isn't structured there at all, it's just a rocket of words, ersatz solutions to the current problems,' said Klaus.
At the presentation of his book, the Czech President stressed that his subject was of great importance for him, and that is why he committed himself as much to this cause.
He also expressed his satisfaction at the fact that his book also appeared in a German translation, since, he said, 'I often meet only the cartoon of my opinions'.
Interested people can now read its positions without being deformed.
Klaus pointed out that although he was not a climatologist, in his view he nevertheless had enough arrangements to draft such a book.
It is indeed an academician capable of monitoring and appreciating scientific treatises, it has the practice of statistics and it is also an economist.
The main conclusions of the book are, according to him, the following arguments: the climate is constantly changing, the changes in it are therefore normal, there is no opinion shared by all researchers on global global warming, the effects of the current changes are neither too great nor too serious, they therefore represent no danger and, finally, the efforts made to influence the development of the climate are useless, inefficient and unreasonably expensive.
All the more in view of the experience of a totalitarian regime, it considers it unacceptable to impose unverified opinions on others, which it perceives as a dangerous attack on freedom.
In response to questions from those present, he repeatedly explained that it was not against "reasonable protection of the environment, but against fundamental environmentalism which does not take into account the relationship between the costs and the profit they bring".
In Vienna, Klaus had already spoken about this subject two months ago at the Hayek Institute.
6 600 000 people did not succeed in renewing their driver's license on time
Six hundred thriving drivers allowed the deadline to expire and did not succeed in renewing their old driving licence, whose validity expires at the end of the year.
This is the last day when people can still apply for a renewal, so that the authorities can send them a new permit before the end of the year.
However, the Ministry was not afraid that hundreds of thousands of unlicenseed drivers would take the road.
According to the authorities, their driving licence has not been re-allocated, especially those who no longer wish to drive.
While the municipalities are pointing a drop in the number of licence renewal applications, the authorities in the capital are overwhelmed by applications, and the number of applications for the renewal of the permit increased this week.
The mayor meets about 2,000 customers per day.
There's a tail of about half an immersion.
In Prague, nearly EUR 140.OOP drivers have not yet revoked their driver's licences.
The issuance of a new permit takes 20 days.
However, it is possible to request the issue within a shortened period of five days.
In this case, the driver pays an additional 500 kroons.
Vehicle drivers who will not be able to carry out the compulsory renewal of the driving licence by the end of the year and take the route without a valid driving licence will risk a fine as from 1 January.
The culprit will be forced to pay a maximum of two thousand kroons.
However, the police officer may also deal with the offence through an amicable approach.
In the case of an administrative offence, the fine rises to DKK 2,500.
According to previous reports, it was likely to be fined even a person who was not travelling.
In so doing, it could be forced to pay up to 30,000 kroons.
The Ministry had called on the authorities for moderation and to impose fines not more than 1000 kroons.
According to Ministry of the Republic of the Ministry of Transport spokeswoman Karel Hanzelka, no one is at risk of fines in the tens of thousands.
