The integration of Slovak cadets in British schools goes badly
The number of children coming from Eastern European countries in British schools is increasing, which is raising concerns about the development of local education.
For the time being, there are a number of obstacles to the integration of Eastern Europeans.
The United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland are even witnessing cases where conflicts between immigrants and the descendants of the inhabitants of origin are turning into violence.
The case of Northern Kent is beginning to accentuate the conflicts remaining with immigrants of Slovak origin illustrates the problem of the integration of children coming from Eastern Europe.
Qurest, baffles and brawls between pupils have been translated into relations between the parents.
The situation had gone so far that the police had arrested in Chatham a ten-year-wrunform student with an iron bar.
The culprit defecated herself by claiming that the boy had insulted him with racist statements.
The police had also had to intervene following an attack on a Slovak immigrant carried out by two adolescent girls.
Girls had allegedly made racist remarks to the man and threw stones at him.
"When we got back to the house, a young person climbed the bus stop and started stowing at us.
When I told him to stop, he answered with apples and pears and then he discovered his buttocks," another unpleasant adventure showed a mother of Slovak students.
Complaints were made by both parties.
One of those who remain went so far as to describe conflicts with immigrants as a struggle for survival.
The cases referred to above show that the atmosphere in British schools between the inhabitants of origin and the immigrants is beginning to be strained.
70,000 Slovaks who came to find work were registered in Great Britain.
And about 30,000 Slovak students attend British schools.
Cuba, the Most Beautiful Island in the Caribbean
For visitors, it really is an island of freedom.
Everything you expect from your holidays can happen if you make a good choice.
Gilded and white beaches such as the snow, high waves or a calm sea like a walnut pond.
Finns and all in-inclusive with waiters giving you pizza on the beach, ice cream or intoxicating cocktails.
Kitesurfing, golf and roulette.
On the islands of the Caribbean Sea, you're not going to find this list just in its entirety, but enriching.
However, there is only one island on which you will still find much more and it is Cuba, the largest and most beautiful island of the Greater West Indies.
In the time of the Spaniards, Cuba had been the bastion of their invasion of America.
Christopher Columbus described it as the most beautiful place the human eye has ever seen.
Hajuna has become the depot and trans-shipment of treasures that the Spaniards had stole and transported in Europe.
Then came the U.S.
The Hostel Nacional was erected in the early 1900s.
It was Lucky Luciano, one of the leaders of the American M Mafia, who built it, since everything that was a crime in the United States (above all prohibited) was accepted and supported in Cuba: drugs, games of chance, prostitution.
Among its customers were, inter alia,
Winston Churchill, Ava Gardner, Frank Sinatra.
In Hajunta, high-height buildings grew and cars, belonging to the cheerful symbols of the gentle ancestry of the last century, were circulating on the wide boulevards.
Ernest Hemingway bought a property in San Francisco de Paula, near Havana, and he had his yacht.
Show business stars and politicians from all over America left their signatures on Bodeguita del Medio.
What about today?
You can leave your autograph yourself on the walls of the Bodeguita in Havana.
Luxury hotel, in the bar of the Hotel Nacional, you'll mix the cocktail of your choice.
And these cult cars are still circulating in Havana, since jigoulis and fiats formerly imported for a long time, in such a savoury and rotten climate.
Although Hajuna, especially the oldest part, is substantially decrepit, nowhere else in the vicinity there are as many splendid colonial monuments as there are precisely there.
Other places are becoming more beautiful, for example Trinidad.
Given that tourism brings money if necessary, its development has the go-ahead.
Over the last 15 years, new hotels have grown not only in Havana, but especially in Varader, on the coast of Holguin province, and also on small islands such as Cayo Coco, Cayo Guillermo and others.
Cuba still proposes a fantasy which is unique in the world and which will be appreciated above all by lovers of small trains.
The cane had re-entered from the extensive plantations in sugar kernels on a narrow track by small steam and engine trains.
Their rolling stock has not been renewed for decades, and this is how small locomotives can be seen in operation, the contemporaneous of whose contemporaries are only in museums.
Where do we steer his footsteps?
The majority of visitors do not miss the best-known places.
So there's no need to write a lot about Varadero or about Havana.
Otherwise, let us recall that in Hemingway property, already mentioned, there is a museum and whoever loves this writer shouldn't miss the visit.
The valley to the west of the island is one of the most scenic corners of Cuba.
Exuberant vegetation grows on limestone soil.
Mogot, lulky balloons and blossom candles from the bottom of the valley, and fertile land having taken hold in their entrails, even its rotten rocks are covered with a very dense plant mat.
The valley is the place where Cuban tobacco of the highest quality is probably cultivated.
You'll be able to meet the final shape of this product if you're visiting one of the cigar factories in Havana.
Someone may also find it interesting that this is where the Chilean Episode of Major 'eman was shot.
Trinidad
Ancient city founded shortly after the Spaniards colonized the island.
It was the slave trade and the sugar trade that gave it glory and wealth.
Restoration was incarnated in the beauty and city-storage character of this smaller city.
Koloni-ln-pan-lsk- architektura, ovlivn-- pozd-j-mi francouz-mi osadn-ky, se za 'ty'i stalet' tempet 'tem' nezm'nila.
Trinidad, located between the sea and the Escambray mountain range, is such an isolated place that, even in the ' 1950s of the last century, there was no road that would connect this region with the rest of the country.
This isolation allowed him to stand aside.
We didn't build anything, and that's why we didnn't stole anything either, everyone just had a roof over their heads.
If we have more time in Trinidad than an afternoon, then it's worth also seeing interesting places in the surroundings, such as Playa Ancan Beach or the Sugarrian Valley.
Then, in the evening, it is customary to go to the Casa de la Musica which animates after the sun sunset and where the thrill only sits in the early morning.
Baracoa
Some refer to this village located in the extreme east of Cuba as the most beautiful on the island.
This opinion is certainly already influenced by the path that leads to it.
The two roads from Guantanamo must cross high and rugged mountains and pass through an almost unmarked landscape of human activity.
United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization (UNCEA), thanks to its unspoiled landscape, was placed on a World Heritage List.
We make the best chocolate, in the church, there is the cross of Christopher Columbus (in reality, it was born about a hundred years after Columbus travel) and from the hill where the old castle-fort and the current El Castillo Hotel stands, we see several mountain tables, the most famous of which is El Yunque.
About coffee, rum, cigars, the Sierra Maestra mountain range, the Bay of sows and the Isla de Pinos Island, the Tropicana cabaret cabaret, the sanctuary of the Fastern Saint of Cuba Caridad del Cobre, Santeria, the African religions practiced by the descendants of the ancient slaves, concerning all this one could fill a number of pages.
Only, as they rightly say, it is better to see once.
In Afghanistan, Allegies are Passive, Americans Say
According to the United United States, the lack of troops and necessary equipment has an impact on the conduct of the mission in Afghanistan.
According to Defence Minister Robert Gates, NATO should outline a concrete plan for the years to come.
The mission in Afghanistan does not proceed as it should, the representatives of the countries with troops in the southern part of the country agreed in Edinburgh in Scotland.
In addition to growing protest on the part of the Taliban movement, the eight States involved are currently also discussing future plans and the coordination of allies.
While the United States, through Defence Minister Robert Gates, through Defence Minister Robert Gates, the United Kingdom sees as a matter of priority the strengthening of the authority of the Afghan government and the intensification of development assistance.
'Developments in Afghanistan are discernible, but military power can only form part of the solution.
The other is to enable the people themselves to start a new development of the economy and of security", the British Defence Minister Des Browne told BBC.
Ninety per cent
The activity of Afghan insurgents, according to United States military data, is the most intense in the last five years.
Violent actions increased by 27 per cent over the past year, up to 60 per cent in Helmand province in the south of the country.
NATO countries have about 40,000 troops in Afghanistan, almost a quarter of them in the southern part.
However, according to the United States, Allies have reached their absolute limit and in the organization and equipment of the troops.
In concrete terms, they call for additional helicopters, soldiers and experts for the training of Afghan troops.
'We will have to think seriously about the concrete objectives for the coming period, as well as on the ways in which we achieve them,' said Gates.
NATO Secretary-General Jaap de Hoop today made similar remarks.
"I don't am happy with what we have on land and in the air in Afghanistan.
We have about 90 per cent of the soldiers and equipment we would need, and this is not enough," De Hoop told Reuters at the end of talks with the Japanese government in Tokyo that lasted three days.
Which is passive
In recent months, there has also been reign between the countries involved in conflicts over the issue if all the allies are indeed doing everything possible in Afghanistan.
For example, Germany was criticized for its passivity.
"The Germans in Afghanistan have a relatively large representation.
But they are engaged in areas where the violence is minimal, so the combat requirements to which they are subject are not as stringent as those in the southern and eastern parts of the country where we operate", said Gates.
The British tabloid Sun this week described the German squad as sluggish.
'Afghan lazy people undiscovered: the Germans are playing, while ours are fighting,' Sun wrote in a document supplemented by photos of German soldiers playing table tennis.
The German newspaper Bild re-attacked by publishing the photo of a British troupe during a rugby match in the southern part of Afghanistan.
The state's debt will rise to one trillion in 2009
The burden of a State debt of more than 84,000 kroons rests with every Czech Republic.
At the end of September, the total debt rose to DKK 842.7 billion.
The government had planned to borrow HKD 159.2 billion this year.
As a result of better-than-expected changes in state budget savings, the ministry has reduced the planned sum to SEK 152.8 billion.
In the end of mid-September, the Government of the Czech Republic amounted to NOK 842.7 billion.
During the nine months of this year it has therefore increased by 40.2 billion kroons.
As of the month of June it fell by NOK 13.4 billion.
Today, the Ministry of Finance has thus confirmed its State debt data from the beginning of October.
The burden of a debt in excess of DK kroon 84,000 rests with every Czech.
Throughout the past year, the state went into debt more than KKM 111 billion.
"For the time being, it seems that, thanks to a less projected deficit for next year, we could only exceed the border by one trillion kroons in 2009.
Next year the debt is expected to be between 900 billion and one trillion kroner kroner", said Patria Finance analyst David Marek for iHNed.cz.
Foresight from Next Finance analyst Vladimier Pikor is similar: 'We could surpass the trillion in the course of 2009.
The precise date will depend on the next budget that is not yet known.
If it appeared that the situation on the political scene was changing, that policies were prepared to save more, the date could be extended to 2010.
But I'm not waiting for this change,' he said.
Disburses
The debt of the State consists of the debts of the central government and is generated essentially by the accumulation of deficits in the State budget.
State debt is financed by treasury bills, annuity securities, direct loans or, e.g., loans to the European Investment Bank.
State domestic idle increased this year from 38.5 billion kroner to KKM 719.4 billion.
The external debt of the State has so far increased from DKK 1.7 billion to N KKM 123.3 billion.
At the end of the month of September, the so-called “trade negotiable State debt reached NOK 803.3 billion,” and from the end of June it fell by NOK 13.7 billion.
The average indebtedness of government debt amounted to 6.5 years.
'Total debt increased inter-announdentally from the first in the third half by 13 per per cent by 13 per per cent, given the current good health of the economy, far too high.
It is of particular interest that the increase is caused mainly by an annuity securities, reaching 20 per cent.
It is bad that the debt is growing, but on the other hand it indicates that there will be room for negotiation when the pension reform comes,' commented Pikora data.
Beautiful than expected
As a follow-up strategy of financing and government debt management for 2007, which the government had planned to borrow 159.2 billion kroons this year.
At the beginning of September, however, thanks to a change in the economies of the State budget rather than expected, the Ministry reduced the planned sum to HKK 152.8 billion.
The sum consists essentially of the deficit in the State budget, which will clearly reach DKK 76 billion this year, instead of NOK 91.3 billion.
Secondly, these are the maximum sources foreseen for the Czech Consolidating Agency (KKM 13.3 billion), NOK 10 billion, the reserve of EUR 10 billion for the disbursement of pensions, the financing of State annuity securities for DKK 43 billion and the redemptions of the European Investment Bank's loans amounting to DKK DKN 1.8 billion.
It appeared today before a court in Khartoum for naming a teddy ber.
The British teacher tried in the Sudan for an insult to Islam was sentenced on Thursday evening by a court in Khartoum to 15 days'imprisonment and expulsion, her lawyer said.
The Briton was accustomed to letting students between the ages of 6 and 7, give the name of Muhammad to a teddy bear when any depiction of the Prophet of Islam is unlawful.
It was punishable by whiplash.
The 54-year-old United Kingdom teacher at the Unity High School in Khartoum in the Sudan was arrested at home on Sunday and charged with insulting Islam and sedition under article 125 of the Sudanese Penal Code, following several complaints by parents of students to the Sudanese Ministry of Education.
The story actually goes back in September, when this teacher asks her students, aged 6/7, to choose a narrow name for the teddy bowl of the class.
'Eight names come out of the mouth of the children, including Hassan, Abdullah and Mohammed,' says school principal Robert Boulos.
Eventually, after a vote in the class, 20 out of 23 children chose Mohamed as their favorite first name.
Mohamed is, of course, the Muslim prophet name, but above all a very common forename in the Sudan.
The children then made a book for the class with the photo of the bear followed by the legend “My name is Mohamed”.
The Bear itself bore no etiquette with this name, but the problem is simply the representation of the Prophet as an insult to Islam.
One of Gillian Gillian Gibbons' Muslim colleagues, a teacher at the same school in the same school in the Sudan, made it clear that he knew her well and that she would never have done anything that might be insulting a religious faith, “I just regret that she had asked the children to vote for a name”, according to him, this is only an “innocent mistake”.
Notice shared by many of the teacher's students who describe it as "very caring towards her students" and "never talking about religion in the classroom".
Remind that Mohamed's first name is also the second most gifted for name in Great Great Britain, it is written in English in 14 different ways.
This name often chosen by Muslim families is a way of honouring the Prophet (often given to the first child of a family).
But what are the rules regarding the use of this name?
For example, can we give it to a toy?
Of course, this is a question of interpretation.
They did not reach an agreement on the future status of the province on Wednesday at the last negotiating meeting in Vienna.
At the end of the day, failure.
The Serbs and Kosovo Albanians concluded on Wednesday morning in Baden, Austria in Baden, a sixth and final sitting of discussions without having been able to overcome their differences on the future status of the separatist province.
'It is regrettable that the parties have not been able to reach an agreement on the statute", European mediator Wolfgang Ischinger said at a press conference in Vienna.
Underlining that peace in the region was “commitable”, its American counterpart, Frank Wisner, urged Belgrade and Pristina to fulfil their commitments to preserve peace and dialogue beyond the end of the negotiations.
'Exctation of no immediate violence is foreseen, but we are entering a very difficult period," he added, noting that 'obvious tensions exist'.
End of mediation on 10 December
Intermediaries of the troika, which includes the European Union, the United States and the United States of America and the United Kingdom, will visit Serbia and Kosovo on one last time on Monday.
The 90%-popula-pounted province could become brighter when Ischinger, Wisner and Russian mediator Alexander Botsane-Khartchenko submit their report to the United Nations Security Council by 10 December.
Discussions in Baden, near Vienna, were the sixth and final negotiating session since the troika was entrusted with further mediation by the United Nations last August.
Americans and Europeans believe that their mediation will end on 10 December.
The Russians, who have already shackled in the Security Council the granting of independence in Kosovo, are insisting on a continuation of negotiations beyond that deadline.
'Spoetry was and remains committed to a negotiated solution, a compromise solution,' said Botsane-Khartchenko.
Unilateral declaration of independence?
After an eight-year United Nations administration in Kosovo, Westerners believe that independence under EU supervision is the only viable solution.
Pristina expresses its readiness to proclaim it unilaterally if the Security Council does not stumble in its favor.
Belgrade insists on greater self-reliance.
“Unhappily, no agreement has been reached,” the President of the Kosovo Autonomous Authority, Fatmir Sejdiu, told the Baden Hotel where these “Last Chance Negotiates have been taking place since Monday”.
Hashim Thaci, a former commander of the Kosovar rebellion and future Prime Prime Minister of the province, expressed his regret.
However, he added that the autonomy plan presented by Belgrade “appeared to a past that led to murders and deportations”.
“Domino  incidence”
Serbian President Boris Tadic, for his part, felt that a compromise was always possible, warning the Albanian majority in Kosovo against the consequences of a unilateral declaration of the independence of the province.
In particular, Belgrade fears a “domino effect” in a region still weakened by the independence wars of the 1990s.
“For Serbia,” he said, “this does not mean that a compromise is impossible.”
On Tuesday, the delegation of Serbia had insisted that any decision should revert exclusively to the Security Council, where its Russian alley had the right of veto.
"Kosovo Parliament will have the last word"
'Only a solution within the Security Council is acceptable for Serbia,' said Serbian Prime Minister Vojislav Kostunica.
"These negotiations lasted two years.
They must come to an end as they have begun: in the Security Council.”
To the journalists, Fatmir Sedjiu had replied that “Kosovo Parliament will have the last word on status”, adding that any unilateral declaration of independence on the part of the Albanian-speakers would be “concordinated” with Westerners.
“Budget plucks an agreement between Israelis and Palestinians”
President George Bush announced, at the opening of the international peace conference convened by him on Tuesday in Annapolis, Maryland, that Israelis and the Palestinians had finally agreed on a joint declaration.
Israeli Prime Prime Minister Ehud Olmert and Palestinian President Mahmoud Abbas agreed to enter into immediate negotiations with a view to finding a final settlement of the 60-year-old dispute between their two peoples.
In the presence of the two men, Bush promised that he would do everything to help them conclude a "Peace Treaty" by the end of 2008.
In the meantime, the Palestinians must dismantle "terrorist infrastructure" and Israel must put an end to its colonization activities, Bush said, reminding both sides of their obligations under the "trafficiate road map", which they accepted in 2003.
The White House leader stressed that the negotiations between Israel and the Palestinian Authority chaired by Abbas would aim "to conclude a peace treaty resolving all outstanding issues, without exception, including central issues".
This name encompasses the three thorny problems at the heart of the dispute between Israelis and Palestinians: the future borders of Palestine, the status of Jerusalem and the fate of Arab refugees expelled from their homes in what is now the State of Israel during the 1948 conflict.
Speaking after the US President, Abbas insisted that the forthcoming negotiations be "global and in-depth" covering all the aspects of a regulation, quoting "Jerusalem, refugees, borders, settlements, water, security, among others".
Momming in the podium, Olmert pledged that the forthcoming talks would be "direct and continuous" and to make an "fridgeable effort in the course of 2008".
At the end of 2008, Bush set the deadline for the implementation of his co-exist "vision of two Palestinian and Israeli states".
Saudi Arabia had revealed that it had secured a commitment in that regard before deciding to participate in the conference.
Arabia, whose participation in Annapolis is a success for D.D., is at the origin of a plan, endorsed by all the Arab countries, which proposes peace to Israel in exchange for its withdrawal from all Arab territories occupied during the 1967 war, including East Jerusalem.
Annapolis marks "a junction in the history of the Middle East, especially because it is attacking the roots of the conflict and its central aspects," Prince Saud said in his interview with the Saudi-owned Arabic-speaking newspaper.
According to the prosecutor, the police are not responsible.
Precisely what happened on Sunday?
As each time in this type of fact, official versions and rumours are at odds.
What are the circumstances of the accident?
According to the main elements of the investigation by the General Inspectorate of the National Police (IGPN), a police car was travelling on Sunday, shortly after 5 p.m. in rue Louise-Michel, in Villiers-le-Bel.
'She wasn't in intervention but on patrol, and was travelling 40-50 km/h, without gyrophare,' one police source explains.
A Kawazaki Green Motorbike, “uncertified and therefore not allowed to travel on the public highway” (see below) and piloted by two youth without helmets, circulated “very fast”.
“She collided with the police car on her left, after a denial of priority to the right,” says a policeman.
The monitoring of blood alcohol on the occupants of the vehicle proved to be negative.
The condition of the car (shunked front, tilted windshield windshield, bump-wires) testifies to the violence of the clash.
Very soon yesterday morning, the public prosecutor Marie-Thérère de Givry announced an investigation for “acts of involuntarily manslaughter and non-assisted persons in danger” at the IGPN, co-checked with the departmental public security director of Val-d'Oise.
In the afternoon, on the occasion of a press conference, the prosecutor stated that the IGPN investigation at this stage precluded the responsibility of police officers.
According to the magistrate, three witnesses of the accident, heard on Sunday evening, confirmed “the version of the police, i.e. that this mini-bike arrived relatively quickly on their left”.
Yesterday, a groove, probably provoked by the rubbing of the pedestals on the ground, showed that the two vehicles remained entangled while the police car managed to brake.
The vehicles have been seized and will be the subject of technical expertise.
The bodies of the two victims were autopsied yesterday.
What happened right afterwards?
Yesterday, a rumor rushed into Villiers-le-Bel, according to which the detectives quickly left the premises, thus not helping the two youngsters.
The IGPN, while remaining cautious, would also rule out “any serious misconduct” in this regard.
'This is a more difficult aspect of the investigation, which requires more investigations, on which we must be very careful,' a police source said.
Libération encountered an eyewitness of these few moments following the accident.
One of its windows leading precisely onto the stage.
'Secondingly, with my cousin who is a nurse in the hospital, trying to resuscitate them, says this witness.
There was nothing to be done."
He then saw 'a completely haggard policeman limping.
Two others were trying to resuscitate one of the young people, the fourth was talking on the phone”.
Faced with the tension that quickly surrounded the scene, and, it seems, to the certainty that those present had of the unexpected situation of the young, the policemen were taken in a car by colleagues who had arrived at the scene.
Still according to this witness, the aid arrived “about 20 minutes after the departure of the police officers”.
They also performed cardiac massages, to no avail.
“I will not say that the police did not provide assistance to young people.
These young people were rescued.
The firefighters arrived right away.
Everything has been done to try to salvage them," the prosecutor said.
Tension rises in Pakistan
Pakistani police repressed the first protests on Monday since General Musharraf introduced a state of emergency two days earlier.
In the city of Lahore, in the eastern part of the country, some 2,000 lawyers who were protesting against the measures taken within the framework of the state of the state of emergency were injured by the tear-infringons used by the security forces, according to witnesses.
They retaliated with jets, infringing, injuring police officers.
At less than 350 lawyers were arrested.
Scenarios similar to Karachi and Rawalpindi, where police officers garnered and injured lawyers.
A number were reportedly questioned.
In Islamabad, Supreme Court lawyers called for a general strike and announced protests, following a wave of some 500 arrests over the weekend of opponents, human rights activists and lawyers.
The streets giving access to the Supreme Court were blocked on Monday morning to prevent the rally.
Other demonstrations reportedly took place in Multan (centre) and Peshawar (north-western).
Thevocal movement on Monday garnered the support of the religious parties grouped in the powerful Muttahida Majlis-e-Amal alliance, the president of which urged the population to send flowers to the judges who spoke out of a state of emergency and garbage to swearing-ets before Musharraf.
