Pervez Musharraf’s promise to leave the head of the army and to become a civilian 'no longer holding' president after a state of a state of emergency, Deputy Information Minister Tariq Azeem said on Monday.
It was thrifting its military uniform by 15 November, the date of the end of its first presidential term.
The Deputy Minister of Public Information also confirmed that the January elections could be postponed.
In addition, the Pakistani Government has resurrected persistent rumours in the country that President Musharraf has been placed under house arrest by his deputy head of the armed forces.
More than one-by-third of pregnant pregnancies remain unexpected
This calculation takes into account undesired pregnancies (24 per cent) and unanticipated or unexpected births.
Even today, more than one in three pregnancies (36 per cent) is unplanned, reveals a study carried out by the National Institute of Demographic Studies (Ined), forty years after the adoption of the Neuwirth Act, which liberalized contraception in France.
This calculation takes into account undesired pregnancies (24 per cent) and unanticipated or unexpected births.
