Spectacular repair on the Space Station
Astronaut Scott Parazynski and his International Space Station (ISS) crew comrades took more than seven hours of space to carry out the spectacular repair of a torn solar panel, which is essential for the continuation of the station's scientific activities.
A risk-already exit that has accumulated records due to its duration, its distancing from the habitable modulus, the speed with which it was imagined, "DIY" tools by astronauts to carry out the repair.
The problem arose on Tuesday when the astronauts unfurled the Harmony module Discovery, a large cylindrical piece that would soon accommodate European and Japanese scientific labs (see box).
This modulus is a kind of decanting dock for laboratory modules.
But we have to be able to supply them with electricity.
The second task of the crew was therefore to move a 17.5-tonne Solar Power Generation Module, already present on the ISS, in order to set it to its final position.
After dismounting it, the crew began the redeployment of solar panels from the module, which have a wingspan of 76 metres.
And that's where one of the panels unfold like accordions, was damaged on nearly a metehicle metehicle by a cable that jammed.
Deployment was halted so as not to further damage the sign.
Astronauts, who were originally expected to carry out an outlet for the Discovery shuttle, followed by repairing another solar panel with a faulty routing system, were forced to conseclose to the damaged panel.
Scott Parazynski was chosen to caracolate at the end of 30 meters in length of the shutt-free arm of the shuttle arm added to that of the station to carry out the repair.
It is one of Nasa's best spacewalker.
Luxurred, very cleavage, it has torso-pronovated and long arms.
At the age of 46, this training physician was therefore instructed to “operate” the “irunted” solar panel.
After precisely inspecting the damage, it severed the plaguing cable and installed some sort of “splits” on the damaged area.
It was aided by a second "spacewalker" remaining near the airfoil and, from the inside, other astronauts including Peggy Whitson, Crew Commander-in-Chief.
The tool he used, an insulator-enveloped hockey mini-gun to prevent electrocution on solar panels producing several hundred volts, was designed and "designed" by astronauts aboard the station.
Upon completion of the operation, Scott Parazynski waited until the sign was fully deployed before making an inventory of its tools and returning to the station.
Yesterday p.m., astronauts were taking advantage of a half-didnance day before seven astronauts left the Discovery Shuttle again on Wednesday.
EADS title sanctioned on the stock exchange
The EADS action is currently declining significantly on the Paris Stock Exchange, after the Aeronautics and Defence Group has announced that it will have to spend between EUR 1.2 and EUR 1.4 billion to deal with the A400M delays.
EADS currently loses EUR 21.96 on the Paris Stock Exchange.
The group was obliged to revise its forecast of operating income for 2007 following the delays caused by the slow increase in the development of the A400M engine, which is a condition for the aircraft's maiden flight.
The cost to compensate for the delay in the delivery of its military transport aircraft, announced in October, amounts to EUR 1.2 and EUR 1.4 billion, more than EUR 1 billion for Airbus, the parent company EADS announced.
As a result of these charges, EADS will be forced to announce new operating income targets (EBIT) for 2007 when presenting its quarterly results next Thursday, it adds without further details.
The group also states that the estimation of the new charges is consistent with the delay of six months.
As a reminder, EADS operating result fell by 86% in 2006, mainly due to costs related to the delays of another programme, that of the very large carrier A380.
Originally, the group planned an initial delivery of the A400M to the French Army in October 2009.
Choosing the status quo
There will be no suspense, on Sunday, at the end of the ballot box: the Russians are going to vote overwhelmingly for President Putin’s party, united Russia.
Confirming their aspiration towards stability, or even authoritarianism, after a highly locked campaign.
Three days before the parliamentary elections, Russian-Russian President Vladimir Putin is now sending voters a televised message about public channels, which has already been recorded but whose content is kept secret.
It is doing so as the leader of the United Russia, the pro-Kremlin party whose victory promises to be overwhelming.
Yesterday, the President did not allow anything to appear by receiving foreign ambassadors, contenting itself with delivering a new charge against the West accused of interfering - its preferred theme during the campaign.
“We know the price of a true democracy and want honest, the most translestive and the most open, elections without snapshots,” he added.
In the eyes of the Opposite, we are far from concerned.
It denounces a campaign tainted by pressure.
The communist leader Guennadi ziuganov said that "never there have been such a dirty election, even in the Yelgite period".
Following the cracking down on last weekend's protests, the former chess World Champion Garry Kasparov, sentenced to five days'imprisonment for an illegal decommunicator, remains banned according to his lawyer.
“The Russians retained the mentality of Hono sovieticus”
Behind these unstudied legislative elections lobbies, there are two much more open-ended issues:
– Who will succeed Putin in the presidency?
The Official Journal yesterday published the date of the first round of the presidential election on 2 March 2008; the nominees have until 23 December to publicise each other.
Elected in 2000 and re-elected in 2004, Vladimir Putin cannot run for a third consecutive term.
However, no successor emerges.
- What role will Putin play after March?
Prime Minister?
Leader of the Parliamentary Majority and the All-Supposed Unified Russia Party who would choose the government and the president?
Be that as it may, in both legislative and presidential elections, outgoing power will triumph.
It is a tradition.
“The Russians have retained the mentality of homo sovieticus, they don’t feel masters of their destiny,” says sociologist Lev Goudkov, director of the Levada Independent Center.
The historian Nikolai Kopossov of the St. Petersburg State University goes further: civil society is lagging behind five-century-old post-European countries; the notorious community spirit has delayed the formation of individualism.
After the liberal reforms of the 1990s, “Putin returned to the model of a paternalist state, perfectly accepted by the population, especially since this new paternalism was not followed by terror, but by a certain degree of economic fluency,” said Maria Lipman of the Carnegie Foundation.
Today, the Russians “governatably relinquish the personal responsibility” they briefly demonstrated after the break-up of the USSR.
'What is the point of thinking?'
The phenomenon affects all social strata, including the business community.
And the administrative elite intends to preserve the status quo: 65 governors (out of 85 heads of Russian regions) are directing the local lists of united Russia.
New freedoms imported from the West are often synonymous with chaos, as in the 1990s.
Public opinion prefers stability.
On behalf of which the Russians will not vote for the extremists either.
Conclusion by Tamara Cherednitchenko, a 54-year-old teacher: ’What is the point of thinking?
Those in power aren't too bad, why change them?"
A lorricious dissertation
After more than 60 years of confidentiality, Bad Arolsen's Nazi archive, retracing 17.5 million victims of the Hillerian regime, have free access.
The 47 million archival documents, stored over 27 km linear in Bad Arolsen (Hesse), have so far been available only for “humanitarian purposes”, i.e. individually and nominatively, at the request of an ex-victim victim or his/her successors.
Eleven States in charge of their management - including Germany, France, Israel and the United States - agreed in May 2006 to open them to researchers and historians.
Since the process of ratification of this agreement by all the Member States has been completed, it has been open since yesterday.
Sensitive information
Historians’ access to Bad Arolsen will make it possible to carry out research “in particular on the movement of prisoners, the camp population and the state of health of the forced labourers”, explains Reto Meistern, the director of these archives managed by the International Committee of the Red Cross (IJRC).
Until recently, a number of States, including Germany, were opposed to the opening to the public of these nominative documents, due to the sensitive nature of certain information.
Like supposed homosexuality or the mental disability of certain victims of deportation.
The registers of concentration camps or arrest warrants prepared by the Gestapo had been collected after the war for practical purposes: to locate civilians who were deported or subjected to compulsive labour and, if necessary, to provide them with proof of indemnity, in order to open the way for their compensation.
Even today in Bad Arolsen, more than 300 permanent employees are processing tens of thousands of individual applications.
Yet, for part of the scientific community, access to Bad Arolsen is of limited interest.
“There is nothing fundamentally new about these documents,” Professor Wolfgang Benz, Director of the Anti-Anti-Anti-Anti-Semitism Research Centre, said in 2006.
The EU and India Want a Free Trade Agreement in 2008
At the present time, bilateral Indo-European trade already amounts to almost EUR 60 billion a year.
The EU is India’s largest trading partner and accounts for 18% of its foreign trade.
The European Union and India are hoping to sign a free-trade agreement in 2008, said EU Trade Commissioner Peter Mandelson and his Indian counterpart, Kamal Nath.
EU Foreign Ministers last April opened the go-ahead to the European Commission to start negotiations to sign Free Trade Agreements (FTAs) with India, South Korea and the Association of Southeast Asian (Asean), and the Indo-European bilateral trade is already worth almost EUR 60 billion a year.
The EU is India’s largest trading partner and accounts for 18% of its foreign trade.
LUE is also its largest foreign investor, but India is only the 9th trading partner of the EU, according to figures from the European Commission.
'A free-exercise pact will divulge the two economies enormously' and 'India would improve its access to an thriving market of 450 million prosperous Europeans,' explained Peter Mandelson.
Europe is the first or the second-largest market for each of the top ten products exported by India, said the European Commissioner; the Asian and European Giants are trading every year for '47 billion euros of goods' and 'zten billions of services', said Danièle Smadja, Head of the European Commission's delegation in New Delhi, regretting that bilateral trade has 'not yet reached its 'potential'.
(Source AFP)
