The Banque d'England said on Thursday was anxious to lighten the current return of concerns about credit conditions, and announced an offer of ten billion Pound4 at the base rate of 5.75% over five weeks, an offer of unexpected duration.
'In order to alleviate the concern that financial market conditions will be particularly tight at the end of the year, and to give banks greater certainty in the manner in which they will organise their liquidity positions over this period, the Bank intends to offer a significant proportion of its expected reserve input during the maintenance period starting on 6 December in the form of a five-half open-progress operation,' she said in a statement.
The bank states that Pound10 billion (EUR 14 billion) will be placed on the market on 6 December at 12h15 GMT, at the base rate until 10 January.
This seems to be the first time that the BoE has put liquidity on the market at five weeks in an open-problem operation.
These normally take place within one week.
The BoE adds in its press release that it "deploys to take further steps to maintain overnight inter-bank loans in line with its base rate.
