In Madrid they will be exhibited on 23 December.
The new gas case between Russia and Ukraine is exposing the alarm.
A quarrel about the price of gas ferments between Ukraine and Russia, thus increasing the risks of repercussions on fuel oil exports to Europe.
Most Russian gas exports to the European Union (EU) are transported through gas pipelines, and each duct is scrupulously monitored.
Kiev has announced that if Russia increases the cost of fuel, it will increase transit taxes to Russia.
Over the past year, an altercation between the two caused a reduction in supplies to EU countries.
The last dispute between Kiev and Moscow came after the Ukrainian government and Gazprom, the gas-supplying Russia's monopolitancy company, had unsuccessfully tried to reach an agreement on the cost of Russian gas supplied to Ukraine for the year 2008.
Gazprom has stated that it will increase as planned, its prices to Ukraine since it has agreed to pay for 2008, up to 50% more for the supply of gas coming from neighbouring Turkmenistan.
Analysts stated that this would include a 40 per four per cent increase in import costs from Gazprom.
“It is essential for us to make use of all our arguments during the conversations, recalling that we are a country through which most of the gas passes,” Ukrainian Economy Minister Anatoly Kinkh told the Reuters News INFORMATION.
“Changes in gas costs would probably be linked to our discussions about transit taxes (to Western Europe) via Ukraine.”
The last major altercation between Moscow and Kyiv caused the interruption of Gazprom supplies destined for Ukraine in January 2006.
This led to a drop in exports to Western Europe, thereby increasing prices.
Russian gas accounts for a one-a-quarter of the European Union supplies.
Russia possesses the largest gas stocks, even though most of them are still underdeveloped.
As a result, Gazprom completed its own supplies with gas from Turkmenistan and other nations of the former Soviet Union in Central Asia.
The Ministry of Health 'must control its foreign employees'.
The Scottish Department of Health needs to improve the controls to be carried out on its extra-Community employees to check whether they have criminal precedents, the Monitoring Committee said.
The Auditor-Administrator, Robert Black, came after a foreign doctor working in Scotia was involved in the Glasgow airport attack in the June Glasgow airport.
Mr. Black also expressed his concern that the Scottish Department of Public Health does not have any specific data on the number of people from the overseas it employs.
Nevertheless, its report highlighted other decisive checks, properly carried out on a sample of employees from five health authorities.
The Auditor-General carried out checks on non-EU personnel following an attack at Glasgow airport.
Bilal Addullah, a 28-year-old doctor of Iraqi origin at the Royal Alexandra Hospital in Paisley, was arrested at the airport after rushing with his jeep into the gates of the main terminal.
It was one of the three doctors who were filmed at Old Bailey in October because they were suspected of being involved in various car explosion attempts in the United Kingdom.
The Ministry of Health will have to carry out pre-employment checks on overseas staff, in order to be certain of the validity of their qualifications, experiences and work permits for the United Kingdom.
Although the Scottish Department of Health uses Scottish Disclosure to carry out the checks of previous criminal studies, the agency has no right to obtain such information outside Great Great Britain.
Among the numerous warnings, the Auditor General's report now states that the committees are required to deal with the Office for the Monitoring of Criminal Prejudicing, thus allowing employers to undertake controls over various countries.
“While we have discovered that certain personnel information has been verified, this is not done every time,” says the report.
Mr. Black stated that the Scottish Department of Health carries out most of the pre-employment checks it would do itself, but he also added: ' our report highlights aspects where the system can be improved in order to ensure that adequate information about some overseas staff is available for health services.
Peter Terry, President of BMA Scotland, said it was "extremely unlikely" that pre-employment controls could identify employees who could commit future terrorist acts.
He stated 'the report suggests that the pre-employment checks currently being carried out, are working well in the Scottish health system and that it is not certain that these warnings could bring any added value.'
The Scottish Sanitary authorities have reported employing 1,161 overseas personnel, most of whom are doctors and nurses.
But the Auditor-General stressed that these figures certainly need to be revised upwards, in part due in part to problems relating to the identification of indeterminous-term staff, who may or may not choose to stay in our country.
The investigation has shown that the current health control procedures in Ayrshire and Arran, Grampian, Greater Glasgow and Clyde, Lanarkshire and Lothian are quite adequate.
These checks are carried out on a passport, work permit and qualifications.
The Scottish Government is currently reviewing its pre-use policy and system of pre-use controls for non-ocean health service staff.
