The climate of Venus could unravel the future of the Earth.
The scientists stated yesterday that Venus has certainly been a pleasant planet to live.
Data from the in-orbiting European ship have shown that the planet is certainly twice as small as the Earth, contrary to what was previously thought.
Venus is the planet closest to the Earth.
These two worlds are practically of the same size, with similar orbits, although Venus is closer to the Sun.
The scientists now believe that billions of years ago, Venus may have had conditions similar to those of the Earth, and might even have been habitable.
But the tweeted effect of carbon dioxide, atmospheric erosion due to solar radiation coupled with slower rotation, led Venus to a completely different kind of evolution.
Today, the planet is deprived of water, is ravaged by Hurricanes and Flash Storms, and has a temperature surface warm enough to melt lead.
The discoveries of Venus Express, the probe of the European Space Agency, which studied the planet over a year, offers a sad view of the potential future of the Earth due to catastrophic changes in the climate.
Univers Oxford University’s Physics Department Professor Fred Taylor, a Venus Express mission leader, said: 'We're beginning to understand why Venus' climate is so different from that of the Earth, although the planets themselves are virtually similar.
“Our new data allow us to establish a scenario that billions of years ago, Venus was originally the same as the Earth, can even be with a habitable surface.”
