The Pain of the Labourists
Gordon Brown uncovered a new staunch; even if he resigned, the situation wouldn't change.
In view of the trend of British scandals, this is not particularly juicy.
No decorations sold, no hotel bills from politicians, no extra-marital twist.
But a revelation shows that a wealthy businessman would have masked for four years, under false names, his contributions to the current Labour Party, equivalent to more than USD 650,000, which the party would not have declared: A new battle that Gordon Brown would have done well.
Two months ago, the Prime Minister went a step backwards in his election project, foolishly insulting British intelligence proclaiming that opinion polls did not influence his decision, and on the contrary he hoped to offer a compact and effective government.
On the other hand, like Vince Cable, the shining liberal democratic leader, stated that the terrifying Mr. Brown had changed from Stalin to Mr Bean.
Purchase of Northern Rock, an inconvenience bank on the verge, demonstrated a lack of guidance and poor emergency judgement.
Improper proposals for a change in the Tax Code provoked a coalition of industrial groups against the government.
The loss of 25 million personal data by the tax authorities revealed an unbelievable administrative blunder.
A trio of rejuvenated generals had launched a revolt among the Lords, protesting against reductions in military spending: they had proclaimed that being Minister of Defence was part-apart-hancy for them, part-time employment.
At the present time, the suspicion of shady donations from David Abrahams; Peter Watt, the Secretary-General of the Labour Party (and his ex-maniferous leader) has had to resign.
None of this means that Mr. Brown is inevitably heading for an exit.
However, governments can come to a point where it would be impossible to govern.
People don't like politicians, and don't have confidence in them, but usually leave their side doubts when a new government comes to power.
It seems obvious that a Prime Minister cannot make a difference or even govern for a long time when he risks suspension: the public administration is no longer functioning; the ministerial offices are taking stock; the press is searching for bad histotories; and the government becomes on the defensive and unobjective.
In one sense, Mr Brown's position is particularly difficult, because unlike other political leaders, he cannot boast of being a beginner.
Although he would like to repudiate Tony Blair, he still was his senior lieutenant.
The Northern Rock problems, and tax data, for example, are related to Mr. Brown's decisions taken when he was Chancellor of the British Treasure.
And even if he is not directly involved in Blair’s misfortunates, such as the party’s long inquiry into the party’s “clusive money for the members of the House of Lords,” voters might think the opposite.
But the difficult situation faced by Mr. Brown is not as tragic as it seems.
The blunder are not so monstrous as members of the Tory party make us believe.
As in Mr. Bean, there are no casualties or scams.
And the remedy, though painful, could be to turn Mr. Brown’s Stalinist tendencies into reality from time immemorial, in favor of a reliable and slightly brutal government.
A good example is the new scandal on political donations.
Mr. Abrahams, a very particular figure, who compares his donations to charity, has stated that he does not want any public recognition of his benevolence.
He may have his opinion, but it is not up to him to decide.
Laws against anonymity of the big subsidizers are clear, and they exist for a very good reason: the public has the right to know which contributes to the success of those who will govern the country.
Mr Watt, who has said that he was aware of Mr Abrahams' programmes, without knowing that it was illegal, will therefore have to be questioned, as will Jon Mendlesohn, the person in charge of the subsidies who had mentioned dubious donations in October.
But so far Mr Brown is not involved: there is nothing to suggest that he was aware of these efforts, even though most of the payments were made as a result of his arrival in government in June.
So far, the Prime Minister has done half.
He quickly condemned the legislative febbluage of his party, but he did not draw attention to the fact that decision-making power rested on something he was familiar with, using a committee of inquiry on the subject composed of a retiring bishop and a judge.
In this case, the lessons to be learned are uncompleted.
While the law has been flouted, it falls within the competence of the police.
In addition, there are many other things that Mr. Brown needs to do, such as finding a permanent chairman for the Independent Committee of the Public Living Standards, without a leader for six months, and preparing a good financial reform of the political parties.
This would oblige the Labour Party, in a moment of weakness, to collaborate with the Tories, which could lead it to abandon its particular financial relationship with confederation; in short, a bitter pill for such a supporter.
But it should confront certain partisan facts.
The Conservative Party now has a good advantage for the elections; it has two years to reclaim the voters, and the only way to achieve a victory is to demonstrate impeccable integrity and strong skills.
