Venus is the infernal binocular of the Earth.
In spite of its unbelief-living atmosphere, Venus looks more to the Earth than previously believed: the European space probe 'Venus Express' has discovered flashes in the atmosphere and vortices at the poles, similar to those that appear in winter on Earth.
For almost three decades, scientists have assumed that there are flashes on Venus.
The European 'Venus Express' probe has today demonstrated this - it has detected electromagnetic waves at the edge of the atmosphere: flashes on Venus burst at a height of about 56 kilometers of cloud-to-cloth, reports Christopher Russell from the University of California at Los Angeles in the scientific magazine "Nature".
Famous cyprine theory, lightning reveals life on the Earth: thunderstorm stimulated reactions in the oceans that led to the formation of amino acids, the cornerstone of life.
This is not the case on Venus: 'While life there has been, it must have been at a very ancient date in history,' explains Sean Solomon of the "Carnegie Institution" in D.C.
There should now be little living things on the surface of Venus: the atmosphere is about a hundred times denser than on the Earth, there are temperatures of 500 degrees.
'Venius may be Earth's infernal binocular, but she remains a binocular,' said Russell.
Why Venus, which resembles the Earth so much in mass and dimensions, was affected by such a different evolution is the fundamental question of the ASE mission.
"Venus Express" in November 2005 was an important milestone in Venusian research, as there had been no probes on their way to the exploration of this planet since the 1990s.
According to the researchers, the most recent research results that have now been presented in several articles in "Nature" do not only highlight the singularity of Venus within the Solar System.
They also show that there is a growing commonality with the Earth of our neighboring planet.
As a result, in addition to turbulent vortices at the north pole, the researchers found, in addition to the wirls of clouds at the south poop, which rotates even faster.
The phenomenon is reminiscent of drood currents in terrestrial hemispheres in winter, writes a team of researchers around Giuseppe Piccioni of Istituto di Astrofisica Spaziale e Fisica Cosmica.
The second planet in the Solar system has almost the same mass as the Earth and is, with 12,100 kilometer-diameter, almost as large.
The planet referred to as an in-night or morning star revolves around the sun in just 225 days.
However, it turns much more slowly on itself: a Venusian day lasts 243 Earth days.
Although Venus, with surface temperatures of 460 degrees Celsius and a pressure of 92 bar, hardly reminiscent of the Earth, scientists assume that the two planets formed under similar conditions.
Of both, the water accumulated and remained on the Earth in the form of oceans, but was lost in space on Venus.
And while carbon-dioxide has been fixed on Earth in the form of carbonate, it constitutes most of the atmosphere on Venus.
