Musharraf reigned for the first time in civilian clothes.
One day after his withdrawal as head of the armed forces, the Pakistani ruler Musharraf was sworn in as president.
Thus eight years of domination by the army ended.
Pakistan President Pervez Musharraf was sworn in for a new five-year term on Thursday.
Pakistan's television aired the ceremonies live at the presidential palace of the capital Islamabad.
Musharraf swore to respect the Constitution and to act for the well-being of the nation.
On Wednesday he handed over his position as head of the army to his confidant Ashfaq Kiyani and blundered international pressure, including the United States.
Eight years after taking power through a bloodless coup, the former general now reigns for the first time as Chief of Civil Status.
By this withdrawal from the post of Commander-in-Chief of the armed forces, Musharraf had fulfilled the main requirement of the Opposition and the West.
Another central requirement is the end of Musharraf's state of emergency on 3 November.
Soon after his Investigation Musharraf will suspend the state of errand, according to Attorney-General Malik Muhammad Qayyum, thereby fulfilling another demand from the West.
The Supreme Court of the country, which Musharraf occupied after the establishment of a state of emergency with jurists of its ranks, had last week rejected all appeals against the controversial re-election of the Head of State on 6 October.
